# Flat Pose Diagnostics at 5 FPS

This notebook checks why the single-mouse model did not clearly improve pose
metrics over the dense triplet model. The key rule is that both checkpoints are
evaluated on the same held-out source windows and the same mouse indices.

## Diagnostic Contract

```text
Dense checkpoint: checkpoints/flat_dense_triplet_5fps/flat_best.pt
Single checkpoint: checkpoints/flat_dense_single_mouse_5fps/flat_best.pt
Window: 16 observed frames -> 24 predicted frames
Rate: 5 FPS
Default sample: 30 triplet windows = 90 paired mouse cases
Seed: 42
Output: outputs/evaluations/pose_diagnostics_5fps/results.jsonl
Per-case rows: outputs/evaluations/pose_diagnostics_5fps/cases.jsonl
```

The diagnostics report:

- matched autoregressive metrics for both variants
- teacher-forced metrics to separate one-step prediction quality from rollout drift
- single-minus-dense paired deltas and single-model win rates
- low/medium/high motion slices from the same selected mouse windows
- Gaussian calibration from sampled bivariate distributions
- dense-triplet attention mass split into within-mouse and cross-mouse context
- exact-step P50/P99 error profiles across the 24-frame prediction horizon
- Gaussian mean versus sampled predictions under both conditioning policies
- horizon-wise 50%/95% ellipse coverage and predicted uncertainty scale
- train-versus-validation motion, position, heading, and local-pose distributions

## Run Diagnostics

```sh
PYTHONPATH=src python -m diagnostics --config src/config/test__flat_pose_diagnostics_5fps.yml
```

## Quick CPU Smoke

Use this while another training run owns the GPU. It exercises the same codepath
on one triplet window and writes to `/tmp`.

```sh
PYTHONPATH=src python -m diagnostics --config src/config/test__flat_pose_diagnostics_5fps.yml --device cpu --max-triplet-windows 1 --seeds 42 --results-path /tmp/pose_diagnostics_smoke.jsonl --case-results-path /tmp/pose_diagnostics_smoke_cases.jsonl
```

